        {
            "question_number": 12,
            "category": "Advanced & Problem Solving",
            "question": "Explain database concurrency control and how to prevent race conditions (such as double-spending or inventory overselling) using optimistic vs pessimistic locking.",
            "why_asked": "Assesses transactional integrity, isolation levels, understanding of database locks, and prevention of critical business data corruption under high concurrent load.",
            "interviewer_looking_for": "Deep explanation of concurrency phenomena (Dirty Reads, Non-repeatable Reads, Phantom Reads). Comparison of Pessimistic Locking (`SELECT FOR UPDATE`) vs Optimistic Locking (`version` column check). Knowledge of distributed locks with Redis (Redlock) when scaling across multiple database shards.",
            "detailed_answer": "1. Race Condition Dilemma: When two concurrent transactions read an account balance of $100 and both attempt to withdraw $80 simultaneously, naive code allows both to succeed because both pass the check before either writes, resulting in balance corruption ($100 - $80 = $20, instead of blocking or overdraft).\n2. Pessimistic Locking:\n- Locks the specific row at the database level during the initial read: `SELECT stock FROM products WHERE id = 1 FOR UPDATE;`.\n- Any concurrent transaction attempting to read that row with a lock is blocked until the first transaction commits or rolls back.\n- Pros: Guaranteed consistency, simple logic.\n- Cons: Risk of deadlocks, reduced throughput under high read/write concurrency.\n3. Optimistic Locking:\n- Assumes conflicts are rare. Adds a `version_id` column to the record. Reads data without locking.\n- During update: `UPDATE products SET stock = stock - 1, version = version + 1 WHERE id = 1 AND version = :current_version;`.\n- If rows affected == 0, a concurrent update occurred. The application rolls back and retries.\n- Pros: Zero lock overhead, high read throughput.\n- Cons: Higher CPU retry cost if contention is extremely high.",
            "code_or_practical_example": "```python\n# Optimistic Locking with SQLAlchemy\nfrom sqlalchemy.orm import Session\n\ndef purchase_item(db: Session, item_id: int, user_id: int):\n    max_retries = 3\n    for attempt in range(max_retries):\n        item = db.query(Inventory).filter(Inventory.id == item_id).first()\n        if item.available_stock < 1:\n            raise ValueError(\"Out of stock\")\n        \n        # Atomic conditional update matching current version\n        updated = db.query(Inventory).filter(\n            Inventory.id == item_id,\n            Inventory.version == item.version\n        ).update({\n            \"available_stock\": Inventory.available_stock - 1,\n            \"version\": Inventory.version + 1\n        })\n        \n        if updated:\n            db.commit()\n            return True\n        # Version mismatch: another transaction beat us. Rollback and retry.\n        db.rollback()\n    raise RuntimeError(\"High contention: purchase failed after 3 retries\")\n```",
            "scenario_example": "Scenario: Flash sale with 10,000 users attempting to buy 50 concert tickets in 1 second. Optimistic locking with retries will overwhelm the CPU. Solution: Use Redis atomic decrement (`DECRBY`) or a persistent queuing mechanism (Kafka/RabbitMQ) to serialize checkout requests, followed by pessimistic locking in worker tasks.",
            "response_structure_star": "1. Concurrency Problem Statement (Lost update & race condition)\n2. Isolation Levels (Read Committed vs Repeatable Read vs Serializable)\n3. Pessimistic Locking mechanics (`SELECT FOR UPDATE`) & trade-offs\n4. Optimistic Locking mechanics (`version` check) & retry loops\n5. Flash-sale high contention strategy (Redis atomic decrements)",
            "what_not_to_say": "Don't claim that wrapping operations in a standard database transaction automatically prevents race conditions. At default 'Read Committed' isolation, read-then-write race conditions still occur without explicit locks."
        },
