        {
            "question_number": 11,
            "category": "Advanced & Problem Solving",
            "question": "How do you design and implement an Enterprise Risk Management (ERM) framework to safeguard an organization against operational, financial, compliance, and reputational risks?",
            "why_asked": "Assesses enterprise leadership, governance maturity, risk appetite definition, and ability to proactively protect business enterprise value.",
            "interviewer_looking_for": "Structured ERM framework (COSO or ISO 31000): Risk Identification, Risk Assessment (Likelihood vs Impact matrix), Risk Response (Mitigate, Transfer, Avoid, Accept), Key Risk Indicators (KRIs), and board-level reporting cadences.",
            "detailed_answer": "1. Establish Governance & Risk Appetite:\n- Define the organization's risk appetite: How much risk is leadership willing to absorb across categories (e.g. zero tolerance for compliance/safety violations vs moderate appetite for commercial innovation risks).\n- Form an Enterprise Risk Committee meeting quarterly with cross-functional leadership.\n2. Risk Identification & Categorization:\n- Operational: Supply chain breakdown, critical IT infrastructure outage, key person dependency.\n- Financial: Currency fluctuation, interest rate surges, customer default/credit risk.\n- Regulatory/Legal: Data privacy (GDPR), labor law shifts, licensing compliance.\n- Reputational/Strategic: Public PR scandal, product safety recall, aggressive competitor disruption.\n3. Risk Assessment & Heat Map Scoring:\n- Score each identified risk from 1 to 5 on Likelihood and 1 to 5 on Financial/Operational Impact.\n- Inherent Risk = Likelihood * Impact. Prioritize the top quartile on an Enterprise Risk Heat Map.\n4. Risk Mitigation & Response Strategies:\n- Mitigate: Implement internal controls, redundancy, and standard operating procedures.\n- Transfer: Procure specialized insurance policies or offload liability via commercial contracts.\n- Avoid: Exit high-risk markets or discontinue hazardous business practices.\n- Accept: Acknowledge low-impact, low-likelihood risks with monitoring.\n5. Continuous Monitoring & Key Risk Indicators (KRIs):\n- Establish KRIs with warning thresholds to detect emerging risks before catastrophic failure.",
            "code_or_practical_example": "```markdown\n# Enterprise Risk Heat Map Matrix (5x5 Scoring)\n- High Impact / High Likelihood (Red Zone - Immediate Action):\n  * Critical single-source supplier disruption -> Action: Dual-source qualification.\n  * Customer database ransomware breach -> Action: Zero-trust architecture & cyber insurance.\n- Low Impact / Low Likelihood (Green Zone - Monitored):\n  * Minor localized office utility outage -> Action: Standard contingency plan.\n```",
            "scenario_example": "Scenario: An organization relies on a single manufacturing supplier in East Asia. ERM identifies this as an unacceptable high-impact risk. The response: Qualify a domestic backup supplier (Mitigate) and purchase business interruption insurance (Transfer).",
            "response_structure_star": "1. Framework Foundation (COSO / ISO 31000 standards)\n2. Categorization across Operational, Financial, Compliance, and Reputational axes\n3. Likelihood vs Impact Heat Map scoring methodology\n4. The 4 Risk Response pillars (Mitigate, Transfer, Avoid, Accept)\n5. Ongoing governance, Board reporting, and KRI monitoring",
            "what_not_to_say": "Don't define risk management as merely buying insurance or avoiding all business risks. Smart organizations take calculated, well-understood risks to achieve high growth."
        },
