        {
            "question_number": 13,
            "category": "Advanced & Problem Solving",
            "question": "How do you calculate and interpret Working Capital, Return on Invested Capital (ROIC), and the LTV:CAC ratio when allocating capital across competing corporate divisions?",
            "why_asked": "Assesses executive capital allocation capabilities, financial modeling literacy, shareholder value maximization, and objective portfolio management.",
            "interviewer_looking_for": "Mathematical and conceptual mastery: Working Capital (Current Assets - Current Liabilities; managing Cash Conversion Cycle), ROIC (NOPAT / Invested Capital vs WACC hurdle rate), LTV:CAC (Customer Lifetime Value to Customer Acquisition Cost; unit economics health). Ability to use these metrics to rank capital investment proposals.",
            "detailed_answer": "1. Working Capital & Cash Conversion Cycle:\n- Formula: Working Capital = Current Assets - Current Liabilities.\n- Interpretation: Reflects short-term liquidity and operational operational agility. A company optimizing its Cash Conversion Cycle (Days Sales Outstanding + Days Inventory Outstanding - Days Payable Outstanding) unlocks trapped cash without taking external debt.\n2. Return on Invested Capital (ROIC):\n- Formula: Net Operating Profit After Taxes (NOPAT) / (Total Debt + Total Equity - Cash).\n- Interpretation: Measures how efficiently a company allocates capital to generate profits. If ROIC exceeds the company's Weighted Average Cost of Capital (WACC), the company creates true economic value. If ROIC < WACC, the business destroys value with growth.\n3. LTV:CAC Ratio (Unit Economics):\n- Formula: LTV = (Average Revenue per Account * Gross Margin %) / Churn Rate. Ratio = LTV / Customer Acquisition Cost (CAC).\n- Benchmarks: < 1.0x (destroying capital), 1.0x-2.9x (unsustainable marketing), 3.0x-5.0x (healthy and scalable), > 5.0x (under-investing in marketing).\n4. Strategic Capital Allocation Decision Framework:\n- When competing divisions request capital: Rank proposals by ROIC spread over WACC, payback period, and unit economic scalability (LTV:CAC). Prioritize projects with high ROIC and demonstrable cash-flow generation.",
            "code_or_practical_example": "```markdown\n# Capital Allocation Division Comparison\n| Metric | Division A (Legacy Retail) | Division B (Digital SaaS) |\n|:---|:---:|:---:||\n| Requested Capital | $2,000,000 | $2,000,000 |\n| Projected ROIC | 8.2% (WACC = 9.0%) [DESTRUCTIVE] | 22.5% (WACC = 9.0%) [ACCRETIVE] |\n| Cash Conversion Cycle | 65 Days | -15 Days (Prepaid annual) |\n| LTV : CAC Ratio | 1.8x | 4.2x |\n| Decision Outcome | REJECT / Self-fund from OpEx | APPROVED: Allocate $2.0M Capital |\n```",
            "scenario_example": "Scenario: Division A generates large top-line revenue but has an ROIC of 6% against an 8% cost of capital. Pumping more money into Division A destroys shareholder wealth. Capital is redirected to Division B, which operates at an ROIC of 24%, increasing overall enterprise value.",
            "response_structure_star": "1. Definitions and formulas of Working Capital, ROIC, and LTV:CAC\n2. Financial interpretation: The ROIC vs WACC value-creation rule\n3. Working capital optimization via the Cash Conversion Cycle\n4. Unit economics assessment with LTV:CAC benchmarks\n5. Portfolio capital allocation matrix comparing competing business units",
            "what_not_to_say": "Don't allocate capital based on which manager screams the loudest or purely on top-line revenue growth without evaluating ROIC or margins."
        },
